// Concept notes: [category, term, description]
module.exports = [
  ["Web and networking", "HTTP as a stateless protocol", "HTTP is a communication protocol in which a client sends a request and a server responds. Stateless means that the server does not remember the content of earlier requests, so each request must carry all the information needed to process it. Because the server keeps no state, any server can take a request, which makes it easy to add servers. Information that must carry over, such as a login state, is kept with cookies, sessions, or tokens.", { why: "If the server had to remember the earlier conversation for each request, the resources needed to keep that memory would grow with the number of users, and a conversation could not continue once a connection was cut. HTTP is defined so that a request can be handled by looking at that request alone, so the server does not have to remember anything between requests.", use: "Receiving web pages and images, a mobile app calling a server API, and a server calling another service's API are all done over HTTP. For features like chat, in which the server also has to send data first at any time, WebSocket is used together with it." }],
  ["Web and networking", "REST", "An architectural style in which resources on the web are identified by URIs, actions on those resources are expressed with HTTP methods, and representations of the resources (such as JSON) are exchanged. Its characteristics are that it is stateless, meaning the server does not store the client's state, and that it has a uniform interface. In practice, an HTTP API designed according to these principles is called a REST API.", { why: "If every API names actions in its own way in the address, such as /getUser and /deleteUserById, people who use the APIs have to learn new rules for each API. REST makes the address point only to a resource and express the action with an HTTP method, so even an API you see for the first time can be read with the same rules.", use: "It is commonly followed when designing APIs through which web screens or mobile apps read and change a server's data, and APIs that are open to the public outside a company. Sometimes GraphQL is used instead to pick only the fields each screen needs, and gRPC to call between servers quickly." }],
  ["Web and networking", "HTTP methods", "The part of an HTTP request that indicates the action the request wants to perform on a resource. GET means read, POST means create or a processing request, PUT means replace the whole resource, PATCH means modify part of it, and DELETE means delete. Use each one according to its meaning so that HTTP behavior such as caching and retries works as expected."],
  ["Web and networking", "Safe methods and idempotent methods", "Safe methods are read-only methods that do not change the state of the server: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, and TRACE. Idempotent methods are methods for which sending the same request several times has the same effect on the server as sending it once: all the safe methods plus PUT and DELETE. POST and PATCH are not guaranteed to be idempotent, so sending one twice can cause it to be processed twice. This is the basis for deciding whether a request may be sent again when no response arrives."],
  ["Web and networking", "HTTP status codes", "A three-digit number that shows the result of the server's handling of a request. 2xx means success, 3xx means a move to another location (redirection), 4xx means a problem with the client's request, and 5xx means an error on the server side. Common ones are 200 (success), 201 (created), 400 (bad request), 401 (authentication required), 403 (no permission), 404 (not found), and 500 (server error).", { why: "If results were reported only as text in the body, each server would write them differently, and browsers, proxies, and monitoring tools could not tell whether a request succeeded. A status code reports the result as a fixed number, so these tools can do caching, retries, and error counting without being adjusted for each server.", use: "It is used to branch handling by the kind of error, such as a frontend that sends the user to the login screen on a 401 and to a \"page not found\" notice on a 404. In operations, you watch the ratio of 5xx responses and, if it suddenly rises, treat it as an outage and send an alert." }],
  ["Web and networking", "Conditional requests and ETag", "A kind of request in which the client asks the server whether the data it already has is still current. An ETag is an identifier value that represents the version of a resource. When the client sends this value in a request, the server responds with 304 Not Modified and no body if the resource has not changed. This reduces network use because the same data is not sent again."],
  ["Web and networking", "URI and URL", "A URI is a string that identifies a resource, and a URL is a URI that identifies the resource by its location (how to access it and its address). Most addresses used on the web are URLs, and every URL is a URI."],
  ["Web and networking", "HATEOAS", "A REST constraint in which responses include links to related resources or to the actions that can be done next, and the client uses the application by following those links. It is part of REST in the strict sense, but real-world APIs often do not apply it.", { why: "If a client writes addresses in its code in advance, the client has to change together with the server whenever the server changes its address scheme. HATEOAS is a way to have the server send links to the next places to go along with the response, so that the client follows links instead of memorizing addresses.", use: "In Spring, the Spring HATEOAS library adds links to responses. In practice, it is common to use it only partly, for example adding next-page and previous-page links to a list response, rather than applying it fully." }],
  ["Web and networking", "HTTPS and TLS", "HTTPS is HTTP communication encrypted with TLS. TLS keeps third parties from seeing the content (confidentiality), keeps it from being changed on the way (integrity), and uses a certificate to check that the server you connected to is the real one (authentication). SSL is the protocol that came before TLS. It is now deprecated and only TLS is actually used, but the name SSL is still used alongside it by habit."],
  ["Web and networking", "TLS handshake and certificates", "The TLS handshake is the process, before encrypted communication starts, in which the client and the server agree on the cryptographic algorithms to use, verify the identity of the server, and agree on the keys that will encrypt the data. A certificate is a document that contains the server's public key and domain information and is signed and vouched for by a trusted certificate authority (CA). The client validates it to confirm that the server it connected to is the real one.", { why: "If you only encrypt and do not verify the other side, you end up in an encrypted conversation even with a fake server that has put itself in the middle. The TLS handshake and certificates let two parties who have not shared a key beforehand meet for the first time and agree on a key, while also verifying that the other side is the owner of that domain.", use: "A server operator gets a certificate from a certificate authority such as Let's Encrypt, installs it on a web server or load balancer, and renews it before it expires. Between internal services, calls are sometimes protected with mTLS, in which both sides verify each other's certificates." }],
  ["Web and networking", "HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3", "HTTP/1.1 handles requests one at a time, in order, on a connection. HTTP/2 sends and receives several requests and responses at the same time over a single connection (multiplexing) and compresses headers. HTTP/3 uses the UDP-based QUIC protocol instead of TCP. This makes connection setup faster and reduces the problem where one lost packet also delays other requests."],
  ["Web and networking", "WebSocket", "A communication protocol that lets a client and a server keep one connection open and send messages freely from either side. In HTTP, the server can respond only after the client sends a request. With this protocol, the server can send data first, so it is used for chat and real-time notifications.", { why: "To imitate a two-way conversation with HTTP requests and responses alone, the client has to keep sending requests, and the headers go along every time even with a short message. WebSocket starts a connection with HTTP and then switches the same TCP connection to WebSocket communication, so both sides exchange short messages without HTTP headers.", use: "In Spring, STOMP support (@MessageMapping) is sometimes used to handle per-topic subscriptions such as chat rooms. When you scale out to several servers, connections are split across the servers, so to send to a user connected to a different server you also use a message relay such as Redis." }],
  ["Web and networking", "SSE (server-sent events) and polling", "Polling is an approach in which the client repeatedly asks the server, at regular intervals, whether there is new data. SSE keeps one HTTP connection open while the server keeps sending events to the client, and data can travel in one direction only, from the server to the client. For cases such as notifications, where only the server needs to send, it is simpler than WebSocket."],
  ["Web and networking", "TCP and UDP", "The two main ways of transporting data on the internet. TCP establishes a connection before communication, guarantees that data arrives complete and in order, and sends data again if there is a problem. UDP sends data without a connection and does not guarantee arrival or order, but in return it is lightweight and fast. HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and database connections use TCP, and real-time video and voice and DNS mainly use UDP."],
  ["Web and networking", "Three-way handshake", "The three-step process used to establish a TCP connection. The client sends a connection request (SYN), the server sends its acceptance together with its own connection request (SYN+ACK), and the client acknowledges it (ACK). After both sides have confirmed that they can send to and receive from each other, data transfer starts.", { why: "If data is sent right away without confirming the connection, it is sent without knowing whether the other side is ready to receive, or where to start counting the numbers attached to each piece. The three-way handshake lets both sides confirm each other's starting numbers in three exchanges, which sets the basis for counting missing pieces and order afterward.", use: "Developers do not call it directly, but the connection timeout is the limit on how long to wait for this process to finish. If a firewall silently drops the SYN, there is no reply and the wait lasts until this timeout, and if no program is waiting on the other side's port, Connection refused appears right away." }],
  ["Web and networking", "Flow control and congestion control", "Both are TCP functions that adjust the sending rate. Flow control adjusts the amount sent to match what the receiver can handle, so that the receiver does not overflow. Congestion control senses how congested the network is and decreases or increases the amount sent, to prevent congestion across the whole network."],
  ["Web and networking", "DNS", "The system that translates a domain name (example.com) into the IP address that computers use to communicate. When you enter an address in a browser, the browser first asks this system for the IP address of that name and then connects to that IP address. Lookup results are cached for a certain time."],
  ["Web and networking", "OSI model", "A model that divides the process of network communication into seven layers by role. From the bottom, they are the physical, data link, network (IP), transport (TCP/UDP), session, presentation, and application (HTTP) layers. Each layer does its own job by using the functions of the layer directly below it. It is the reference for calling the location of a device or a problem \"Layer N\"."],
  ["Web and networking", "Web server and application server", "A web server is a program that receives HTTP requests and either responds with static files such as images and HTML or forwards the requests to another server (Nginx, Apache). An application server (web application server) is a program that runs program code and builds a result for each request (Tomcat). A common setup puts a web server in front and passes requests on to the application server.", { why: "If one application server takes on static file delivery and TLS handling in addition to running code, then every time you scale the application server to several machines or replace it, these settings also have to be adjusted on each server. If you put a web server in front, the shared work can be handled there, and the application server can concentrate on running code.", use: "A Spring Boot web application runs on embedded Tomcat by default, so it is itself an application server. In the cloud, a setup in which a load balancer or CDN takes TLS handling and static file delivery instead of a web server is also common." }],
  ["Web and networking", "Reverse proxy", "A server that receives client requests on behalf of internal servers, forwards them to those servers, and returns their responses to the client. The client does not know the actual servers directly. Its roles include distributing requests across several servers, handling TLS, caching, and protecting the internal servers.", { why: "If slow clients connect straight to an application server, the request processing threads stay tied up with those connections while the clients slowly upload a large request body or slowly take a response. A reverse proxy such as Nginx, in its default configuration, buffers requests and responses and exchanges data quickly with the backend server, so the backend server's threads are not tied up for long by slow connections.", use: "It is used to split static file requests and /api requests by path and send them to different servers, or to reject too-large uploads at the front with Nginx's client_max_body_size. In Kubernetes, an Ingress controller takes this role." }],
  ["Web and networking", "Tomcat's threading model", "Tomcat manages its request processing threads in a pool, and one thread takes one request and handles it until the response is finished. The number of requests it can handle at the same time is limited by the maximum number of threads (200 by default). Unlike the standard Java ThreadPoolExecutor, when no thread is idle it adds threads up to the maximum first, and new requests wait only when all of them are in use. A thread stays occupied even while the request is waiting on a database or an external call.", { why: "If one thread handles a request all the way through, even code that waits for a database response can be written from top to bottom in order, without being split into callbacks. Tomcat takes those threads from a pool created in advance and sets an upper limit on their number, so threads do not grow endlessly even when requests pile up.", use: "A Spring MVC service with slow external calls or many concurrent requests tunes server.tomcat.threads.max together with the size of the database connection pool. When requests seem to be stuck, you look in a thread dump to see at which line a request processing thread such as http-nio-8080-exec-1 is waiting." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Scale up and scale out", "Scaling up means raising the specifications (CPU, memory) of a single server to increase its capacity, and scaling out means adding more servers to share the load. Scaling up is simple but has limits and cost problems. Scaling out can grow flexibly, but the servers must be designed to hold no state, and you need something that distributes the requests."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Load balancer", "A device that distributes incoming requests across several servers. It checks the state of the servers periodically (health check) and does not send requests to a server that has a problem. It keeps load from piling up on one server and keeps the service running even when some servers fail."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancers", "A Layer 4 load balancer distributes requests by looking only at transport layer information such as IP addresses and ports. A Layer 7 load balancer can distribute requests by looking at HTTP content such as the address, path, and headers, so it can do fine-grained handling such as sending requests to different server groups depending on the path.", { why: "If all distribution were done by reading even the HTTP content, there would be a cost of interpreting every request, and if only the IP and port are looked at, /api and /images cannot be sent to different servers. The split into Layer 4 and Layer 7 exists so that you can choose whichever you need, fast distribution or distribution by content.", use: "In AWS, NLB is a Layer 4 load balancer and ALB is a Layer 7 load balancer. Non-HTTP connections such as a database or a game server are handled at Layer 4, and web APIs that must be sent to different services by path or host name are handled at Layer 7." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Autoscaling", "A feature that automatically increases or decreases the number of servers according to metrics such as CPU utilization or request count. It adds servers when load is high and removes them when load is low to save cost. It takes time for an added server to become able to take requests, so for a surge that is known in advance it is safer to add servers ahead of time.", { why: "If you fix the number of servers for the busiest time, you pay even in quiet hours, and if you fix it for ordinary times, the system cannot hold up when requests surge. Autoscaling changes the number of servers as needed according to metrics, so you do not have to give up either one.", use: "An AWS Auto Scaling group or a Kubernetes HorizontalPodAutoscaler adjusts the number of servers or Pods according to CPU utilization or request count. It is commonly used in shopping services and delivery services where usage differs greatly during the day." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "TPS and response time percentiles", "TPS is the number of requests (transactions) a system handles in 1 second. For response time, the average hides the few slow requests, so you look at percentiles. A p95 of 1 second means that 95% of requests are handled within 1 second, and p99 is the same measure taken at 99%. The few slow requests shape the user experience, so manage p95 and p99 together."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Load testing", "Testing that artificially sends a volume of requests similar to real traffic to find out how much the system can withstand and which part becomes the problem first when it reaches its limit (the bottleneck). It is used before going live to learn the capacity the system can handle and to measure the effect of improvements. Tools such as JMeter, nGrinder, and k6 are used.", { why: "A functional test only checks whether a single request is handled correctly, so it does not reveal problems such as a connection pool that runs short or lock waits that get long when hundreds of people use the system at once. A load test is a way to deliberately reproduce such problems before users run into them.", use: "It is used before a large sale event or a service launch to check whether the system withstands the expected number of concurrent users and to decide how many servers are needed. To avoid touching production data, it is run in a separate environment with specs similar to production." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Bottleneck", "The slowest part of a system, which limits the processing performance of the whole. No matter how much you improve the other parts, overall performance does not improve while this part stays the same. Performance improvement starts with finding it by measurement."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Rate limiting", "Limiting the number of requests that are allowed in a given period of time. It stops a specific user or client from sending excessive requests and affecting the whole system, and it makes the system accept requests only within the range it can handle. Requests over the limit usually get a response with the 429 status code."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "CDN", "A network that keeps copies of content on servers placed in many regions around the world and has a server close to the user respond. Static files such as images, video, and scripts are delivered faster, and fewer requests go to the origin server."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Monolithic architecture and microservice architecture", "A monolithic architecture builds all features as one application and deploys them together. A microservice architecture splits the features into small services, and each one is developed, deployed, and scaled independently. With microservices, each service can be deployed and scaled separately and failures are isolated, but the complexity of communication between services, data consistency, and operations grows a lot.", { why: "An application that has grown to hold many features has to be rebuilt and redeployed as a whole even for a small change, and several teams that share the same code and deployment schedule end up waiting for each other. Microservice architecture is a way to split the system into services so that each team changes and releases its own service separately.", use: "Organizations in which several teams share the work on a large service use microservice architecture. Small teams and new services often start as a monolith and split off services when the boundaries to split on become clear and the need arises." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Distributed transactions and the saga pattern", "The problem of handling work that spans several services (or databases) as if it were a single transaction. When each service has its own database, the work cannot be wrapped in one local transaction. Two-phase commit (2PC) can tie several databases together, but its burden on availability and coupling is heavy, so it is rarely used in microservices. In the saga pattern, each service commits its own work in turn, and if a step fails partway, compensating transactions that cancel the already completed work are run to restore consistency."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Compensating transaction", "A separate operation that is run to undo the effect of work that has already been committed. Unlike a database rollback, it is a new transaction that performs the opposite action (for example, canceling a payment or restoring stock). In the saga pattern, it is used to cancel the earlier steps when a failure occurs.", { use: "Even in an application that has only one service, when saving to its own database fails after it has called an external payment API, it calls the payment cancellation API to bring things back in line. Because a compensating action can also fail, it is retried or left on a list for a person to handle." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Transactional outbox pattern", "A pattern for making a database change and a message publication succeed together. Instead of publishing the message directly, the application saves the message to be sent in a separate table (the outbox) in the same transaction that changes the business data. Later, another process reads this table and publishes the messages to the message broker. This prevents the inconsistency where the change is in the database but the message was never published.", { use: "It is used where an event must be delivered to another service after a database save, such as a shopping mall where the inventory and shipping services must be notified once an order is saved. Because the publishing side may send the same message again, the receiving side must filter out what it has already processed by message ID." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Circuit breaker", "A mechanism that blocks calls to a service that has failed, so that callers do not keep calling it and waiting. When failures pass a certain level, it moves to the open state, which blocks calls, and makes later calls fail immediately. After a certain time, it lets some calls through as a trial to check whether the service has recovered. It stops a failure in one service from spreading to the services that call it."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "API gateway", "The single entry point that receives all client requests first and forwards them to the right internal service. It handles common functions such as authentication, rate limiting, and logging in one place. Clients do not need to know the layout and addresses of the internal services."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Strangler fig pattern", "A way to replace an existing system gradually by moving features to a new system one at a time, instead of replacing it all at once. At the front, where requests arrive, only the requests for features that have been moved go to the new system, and the existing system keeps handling the rest. When all features have been moved, the existing system is removed.", { use: "It is used when splitting an old monolithic service into several services, or when replacing a screen built with an old framework with a new technology. New features are built in the new system from the start, and existing features are moved starting with those that are used less or are easy to detach." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Layered architecture", "A structure that divides code into layers by role. It is usually divided into the presentation layer (controllers), which handles requests and responses, the application layer (services), which coordinates the business flow, the domain layer, which holds the business rules, and the infrastructure layer, which handles the database and external integrations. Each layer focuses only on its own role and depends only in the defined direction.", { why: "If request interpretation, business rules, and SQL are mixed in one method, then changing just the response format or the database means searching through and fixing even the code that holds the business rules. Layered architecture divides code by role, so that code that changes for the same reason is gathered in one place.", use: "It is a basic structure commonly used in Spring web applications, and the packages divided into controller, service, and repository, and the @Controller, @Service, and @Repository annotations follow this division." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Clean architecture and dependency inversion", "A structure that puts the business rules (the domain) at the center and makes technical details such as the database and the web framework depend on the domain. The domain knows nothing about the outside technology. This direction is kept by dependency inversion: the features the domain needs are defined as interfaces, and the outer layers implement them. Changing the technology does not affect the business rules.", { why: "If a service's business rules call technical code such as JPA or a payment provider's SDK directly, changing the technology means changing the business code too, and it is also hard to test the business rules on their own. Clean architecture reverses the direction of dependency so that the business code knows only the interfaces it defined itself, which makes it possible to test the business rules separately without the technology.", use: "It is used in services whose business rules are complex and long-lived, or in which an external technology such as a payment provider or a message broker may be replaced. For work that is mostly simple reading and saving, the extra layers and interfaces are more of a burden, and a layered structure is often enough." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "IaaS, PaaS, SaaS", "A classification of cloud services by how much they provide. IaaS rents out infrastructure such as servers, storage, and networks, and the user manages the operating system and programs on top of it. PaaS also provides the environment that runs the application, so the user only uploads code. SaaS lets you use finished software over the internet.", { use: "This is the classification used to decide how much you manage yourself. If you must touch the operating system, you use IaaS such as AWS EC2. If you want to upload only code, you use PaaS such as Heroku or AWS Elastic Beanstalk. For things you do not need to build yourself, such as mail or messengers, you use SaaS such as Gmail and Slack." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "On-premises and cloud", "On-premises means that a company buys its own server hardware, keeps it in its own facility, and operates it. Cloud means renting a cloud provider's resources as needed and paying for what you use. The cloud lets you add and remove resources quickly with no upfront investment, and on-premises gives you direct control over the hardware and the data.", { use: "A new service or a service whose usage fluctuates is often started in the cloud. In finance and the public sector, where it is hard to keep data outside the company, and in places that already have the equipment, on-premises is used or the two are mixed." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "VPC and subnets", "A VPC is an isolated virtual network that you create inside the cloud. A subnet is a section that divides the VPC into a smaller address range. A common setup places load balancers in a public subnet, which can be reached directly from the internet, and application servers and databases in a private subnet, which cannot be reached directly from outside.", { why: "If every server in the cloud is given a public address and left open to the internet as it is, even the database server can be reached by connection attempts from outside. A VPC creates your own private network inside the cloud and lets you decide separately, for each subnet, the route to the outside.", use: "It is used as the unit that decides which network to place servers in when launching them on a cloud such as AWS or GCP. If a server in a private subnet must call an external API, it goes out through a NAT gateway, and the ports to open on each server are set with security groups." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Containers and images", "A container is a technology that packages an application with the things it needs to run and runs it in an environment isolated from other processes, while sharing the kernel of the host operating system. An image is a read-only template used to create a container, and it holds the application, libraries, and configuration. Running the same image greatly reduces problems caused by differences between environments."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Containers versus virtual machines", "A virtual machine virtualizes hardware and runs a whole separate operating system installed on top of it. A container shares the operating system kernel of the host and isolates only the processes. So a container is lighter and starts faster than a virtual machine, but a virtual machine has a higher level of isolation.", { use: "To run several applications lightly on the same Linux, use containers. To run a different operating system or to isolate untrusted code strongly, use virtual machines. In the cloud, running containers on top of virtual machines is a common setup." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Namespaces and cgroups", "Linux kernel features that are used to implement containers. Namespaces separate what a process can see (the process list, the network, the file system, and so on) so that each one looks like an independent environment. cgroups limit the amount of resources, such as CPU and memory, that a group of processes can use.", { why: "Processes on the same server can see each other's process lists and the ports in use, and when one uses up all the memory, other processes can slow down or be killed. Namespaces and cgroups let the kernel set separately, for each group of processes, what is visible and how much can be used, which reduces this interference.", use: "Docker and Kubernetes use them when they start containers. The --memory and --cpus options of docker run and Kubernetes' resources.limits become cgroups settings, and the JVM reads this memory limit to set the default heap size." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Multi-stage build", "A way of building a Docker image that separates a build stage from a stage that runs the application. Only the output compiled in the build stage is copied into the image of the stage that runs the application, so build tools and intermediate files are not included in the final image and the image gets smaller.", { why: "Before multi-stage builds existed, people kept separate Dockerfiles for building and for running and moved the output with a script, or used a large image that still contained the build tools. A multi-stage build lets you write the two stages in one Dockerfile and keep only the last stage as the image.", use: "A Java application builds the jar with the JDK and Gradle or Maven in the first stage and, in the last stage, copies the jar into a small image that has only the JRE. A small image shortens the download time at deployment and reduces what has to be checked in security scans." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Kubernetes", "A platform that automates placing and managing containers across several servers. You declare a desired state (for example, run 3 copies of this application), and it keeps adjusting so that the actual state matches it. It restarts containers that die, adjusts their number according to load, and replaces them with a new version step by step."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Pod, Deployment, and Service", "The basic building blocks of Kubernetes. A Pod is a group of one or more containers that run together and is the smallest unit of deployment. A Deployment manages how many Pods to keep and how to replace them with a new version. A Service provides an unchanging access address for Pods that change often, and it distributes requests among them.", { use: "When you deploy a Spring Boot application to Kubernetes, you usually write one Deployment and one Service in YAML and apply them with kubectl apply. Other services call it by the Service name, and outside requests reach the Service through an Ingress." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "CI/CD", "CI (continuous integration) means merging code changes often and automatically running a build and tests on every merge to find problems early. CD (continuous delivery or continuous deployment) means using an automated process to get verified code into a deployable state or to deploy it all the way to production. Automating the build, test, and deployment work that people used to do by hand reduces mistakes and shortens the release cycle."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Rolling, blue-green, and canary deployments", "Ways to deploy a new version without interrupting the service. A rolling deployment replaces servers with the new version a few at a time, in turn. A blue-green deployment prepares a complete, separate environment for the new version and then switches the traffic all at once. A canary deployment applies the new version to only some users first, checks that there are no problems, and then expands it gradually."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Graceful shutdown", "Stopping an application by accepting no new requests, finishing the requests that are already in progress, and only then exiting. It prevents errors caused by in-progress requests being cut off when a server is taken down, for example for a deployment.", { use: "Spring Boot sets this behavior with the server.shutdown setting (since Spring Boot 3.4 the default is graceful, so you do not need to turn it on; set immediate to turn it off) and sets the maximum time to wait with spring.lifecycle.timeout-per-shutdown-phase. Kubernetes sends SIGTERM when it takes a Pod down and ends it forcibly after a set time, so you adjust things so that cleanup finishes before then." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Health check (liveness, readiness)", "A periodic check of whether a server is healthy. Liveness checks whether the process is alive, so that it is restarted if there is a problem. Readiness checks whether the server is ready to take requests, so that traffic is not sent to a server that is not ready. This keeps requests from reaching a server while it is starting up or shutting down.", { why: "If you do the check in only one way, the server may be restarted just because the database was briefly slow, or conversely requests keep being sent to a server that cannot take requests. Separating liveness and readiness lets you decide separately whether to restart it and whether to send requests.", use: "Have Kubernetes' livenessProbe and readinessProbe call Spring Boot Actuator's /actuator/health/liveness and /actuator/health/readiness, and for an application that takes a long time to start, add a startupProbe so that it is not restarted while it is starting." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Observability (logs, metrics, traces)", "The ability to understand the internal state of a system from its external outputs alone. Logs are records of individual events, metrics are values aggregated as numbers, such as request counts and response times, and traces are records of the path one request takes through several services and the time spent in each segment. You need all three together to detect problems and find their causes."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "Distributed tracing (trace ID, span)", "A technique for tracking the whole path of a single request as it is processed across several services. A unique trace ID is assigned when the request first arrives, and it is passed along with every call between services. The segment handled by each service is called a span, and collecting the spans with the same trace ID shows which segment took the time."],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "APM", "A tool that monitors the performance of an application. It collects and shows the response time of each request, which methods or queries took the time, the errors that occurred, and more. In Java, it is usually applied as an agent, with no code changes. Examples are Pinpoint, Scouter, and Datadog.", { why: "To find the cause of slowness from logs alone, you have to repeat the work of adding code that measures time at each suspect place and redeploying. An APM keeps collecting the time spent in methods, queries, and external calls from the running application, so you can look at the record from that time even after a problem has occurred.", use: "It is used when a certain API suddenly becomes slow in production: you pick one slow request and trace which query or external call took the time, and compare response times and error rates before and after a deployment." }],
  ["Architecture and infrastructure", "MDC", "A logging feature that automatically prints context information about the current request (such as the request ID and the user ID) with each log entry. It keeps values per thread: if you put the values in when a request starts, the same values appear in every log entry written by the thread that processes that request. Even when the logs of many requests are mixed together, you can pick out the logs of a single request.", { why: "If you attach a request ID to each log statement by hand, some places get missed, and there is no way to attach it to the logs that libraries write. MDC makes the log format print the request ID, so that the same value appears on every line without changing the code that writes the logs.", use: "In a filter, you set the request ID with MDC.put when a request comes in, and print it with %X{key name} in the Logback or Log4j2 log format. When logs from several servers are gathered in one place for searching, you use this value to see the logs of one request together." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Design pattern", "A proven solution to a problem that comes up repeatedly in software design, given a name and written down. These solutions serve as a shared vocabulary among developers, so a design intention can be communicated briefly. Each one is a means of solving a specific problem, so applying one where it is not needed only adds complexity.", { use: "They also appear often when you read Spring code. JdbcTemplate follows the Template Method pattern, the way @Transactional works follows the Proxy pattern, and the structure that receives events with @EventListener follows the idea of the Observer pattern." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Singleton pattern", "A pattern that guarantees that only one instance of a class is created and lets code access that instance from anywhere. It is used for objects that must exist only once, such as configuration information or connection management. In Spring, instead of implementing it yourself, the container creates and manages one object per bean definition (this differs from the GoF Singleton, which guarantees one instance per class).", { why: "If you create an object that needs to exist only once with new everywhere it is needed, resources are wasted, and each object ends up with a different state. The Singleton pattern blocks the constructor with private and returns the same instance through a static method, so everyone shares one.", use: "It can be seen in libraries, such as Java's standard Runtime.getRuntime(). Implementing it yourself makes it hard to replace with a fake in tests, so in a Spring application you register it as a bean and have the container create just one and inject it." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Strategy pattern", "A pattern that defines an algorithm or behavior that may change as an interface and implements each variant as a separate class, so that it can be swapped at run time. The code that uses it depends only on the interface, so adding a new variant does not require changing the existing code."],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Template Method pattern", "A pattern in which the superclass fixes the overall flow of a task and subclasses implement only the steps within that flow that differ. It removes duplication of the common procedure. Spring's JdbcTemplate is built on the same idea: it fixes the flow of getting a connection, handling exceptions, and cleaning up resources, and it takes only the varying part as an argument.", { why: "If several classes each implement the same sequence of preparation, a differing step, and cleanup on their own, a mistake of forgetting the cleanup step can occur in each class, and changing the sequence means changing all of them. The Template Method pattern puts the sequence in one place, the superclass, and so prevents such mistakes.", use: "Java's standard AbstractList provides the rest of the behavior once you implement only get and size, and in the Servlet API, the service method of HttpServlet calls doGet or doPost depending on the request method. When an inheritance structure is a burden, you can instead pass only the part that varies as a lambda." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Builder pattern", "A pattern for creating an object by setting the needed values one at a time through methods and then creating the object at the end. When a constructor has many parameters or many optional values, the code shows which value is which, so it is easy to read, and you can specify only the values you need.", { why: "If a string of numbers and null follows new, you cannot tell which position is what, and if you fill in values one by one with setters, an object that has not been completely filled in can be used in the middle. The Builder pattern makes the object only after all the values are set, which avoids both problems.", use: "It is often seen in libraries, such as Java's standard HttpRequest.newBuilder() and Spring's UriComponentsBuilder. When you write one yourself, Lombok's @Builder is sometimes used to generate the builder code automatically." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Unit tests and integration tests", "A unit test verifies the behavior of a single method or class in isolation from other parts. It runs fast and makes the cause of a failure easy to find. An integration test actually connects several components (such as the Spring container and a database) and verifies that they work together. It is closer to reality but slow. The usual approach is to have many unit tests and to keep integration tests focused on the core flows."],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Test double (mock, stub)", "A test-only object that stands in for a real object that the code under test depends on. A stub is made to return fixed values, and a mock can also verify which methods were called and how. They let you verify only the logic of the code under test, quickly and consistently, without a database or an external API.", { use: "Use Mockito's mock() or @Mock to replace a repository with a fake, when(...).thenReturn(...) to set the value to return, and verify(...) to check whether it was called. In Spring tests, @MockitoBean (formerly @MockBean) replaces one bean with a fake." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Test coverage", "The proportion of the whole code that is executed when the tests run. It is useful for finding code that is not tested. However, code being executed is not the same as its result being verified as correct, so a high number does not guarantee the quality of the tests."],
  ["Development practices and tools", "TDD (test-driven development)", "A development approach in which you write the test that verifies a feature before you implement the feature. You repeat a short cycle in this order: write a failing test, write the minimum implementation that passes the test, and clean up the code (refactoring). It steers the design toward a structure that is easy to test and gives you a safety net for changes.", { why: "If you implement everything and write the tests afterward, the tests are easily left out under schedule pressure and tend to be written to fit the code already written. TDD reverses the order and has you write the expected behavior as code first, so you decide what to build and then implement only that much.", use: "It fits discount and settlement logic with complex calculation rules, and when fixing a bug, you also write the failing test that reproduces the bug first and then fix it. It is used less for things that must be judged by eye, such as screen layout." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Refactoring", "The work of improving the internal structure of code while keeping its observable behavior the same. It makes code easier to understand and change by removing duplication, improving names, splitting long methods, and so on. To do it safely, you need tests that confirm the behavior has not changed.", { why: "If you only keep adding features, the code gets more tangled, so even a small change takes a long time and bugs increase. Refactoring separates the work of fixing only the structure while keeping behavior the same from adding features, and has you do it in small steps, often.", use: "You do it when you first tidy the code into a structure that makes a new feature easy to add before adding it, and when a code review points out that the code is hard to read. Using IntelliJ's automatic refactorings such as Rename and Extract Method reduces mistakes in renaming and splitting methods." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Git fetch, pull, and push", "The fetch command downloads new changes from the remote repository but does not apply them to your working branch. The pull command does a fetch and then also merges the downloaded changes into the current branch. The push command uploads your local commits to the remote repository.", { why: "A central repository system like SVN puts a commit on the server right away, so you cannot commit without a network, and others can take the commit as soon as you make it. Git first keeps the commit in the repository on your own computer, and lets people decide when to exchange with the remote repository using fetch, pull, and push.", use: "At the start of work, you get the team's latest changes with pull, and when the work is finished, you push and then open a Pull Request. To see the differences before merging, you fetch and then check with git log or git diff." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Merge and rebase", "Two ways to combine the changes of two branches. A merge joins both histories as they are (if the histories have diverged, a merge commit is created, and if they have not, only the branch pointer moves forward). A rebase moves your commits to after the latest point of the target branch and applies them again, which makes the history a single line. A rebase re-creates existing commits as new commits, so do not use it on commits that you have already shared with others."],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Merge conflict", "A situation in which two branches changed the same part of the same file in different ways, so Git cannot combine them automatically. A person looks at the parts marked as conflicting, decides which side to keep or how to combine the two, edits the file, and then completes the merge."],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Git-flow and GitHub Flow", "Strategies for managing branches. Git-flow keeps a branch for releases and a branch that integrates development, and it manages features, release preparation, and urgent fixes on their own separate branches. It suits products that release versions on a fixed schedule. GitHub Flow is a simple approach in which you create a short-lived working branch from a single base branch, merge it after review, and deploy right away. It suits services that deploy at any time.", { why: "Without branch rules, people create branches by their own standards, and it becomes unclear which branch is what is deployed now and where to branch an urgent fix from. Git-flow and GitHub Flow decide in advance the kinds of branches and the direction of merging, so the team works in the same order." }],
  ["Development practices and tools", "Code review and pull requests", "A pull request (PR) is a request to merge the changes on a working branch into the base branch. Code review is the process in which other developers examine the changes before they are merged. It finds defects early, keeps the code consistent, and shares knowledge within the team.", { why: "If everyone pushes changes straight to the base branch, others use that code without knowing what changed, and mistakes show up only after they reach production. A pull request adds a step before merging in which you see the changes on one screen, exchange opinions in comments, and check the automated test results.", use: "On GitHub and GitLab (where it is called a Merge Request), you protect the base branch so that only changes with review approval can be merged, and sometimes a CODEOWNERS file automatically sets who reviews each path. So that reviews do not drag on, split the work so that one pull request is a small change of about one feature." }],
];
